      "culturalNotes": [
        "In football/soccer scores, English speakers say 'nil' for zero, never 'zero' — 'two-nil' (2-0), not 'two-zero'. This is sport-specific; outside of scores, zero is the normal, all-purpose word.",
        "British speakers sometimes use nought for zero in everyday or slightly old-fashioned contexts (e.g. 'nought point five' for 0.5), while American English almost always says zero or oh instead."
      ]
