    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "The **Present Simple** describes habitual actions, general truths, and permanent states. In English, conjugation is identical for almost all persons except **HE, SHE, and IT (The 3rd Person Singular)**.\n\n1. **The Present Simple Conjugation Matrix:**\n- **I / You / We / They:** Use the **Base Verb** (*I work, you live, we speak, they learn*).\n- **He / She / It:** Add **-S / -ES / -IES** to the base verb (*He works, She lives, It rains*).\n\n2. **Spelling Rules for He/She/It:**\n- **General rule:** Add **-s** (*work -> works, speak -> speaks, live -> lives*).\n- **Verbs ending in -ch, -sh, -ss, -x, -o:** Add **-es** (*watch -> watches, wash -> washes, pass -> passes, fix -> fixes, go -> goes, do -> does*).\n- **Consonant + -y:** Change *-y* to **-ies** (*study -> studies, fly -> flies, carry -> carries*).\n- *(Vowel + -y just adds -s: play -> plays, buy -> buys)*.\n\n3. **The 3 Pronunciation Rules for Final -S:**\n- **Rule 1 (/s/):** After voiceless consonants /p, t, k, f, θ/ -> *works* [wɜːks / WURKS], *speaks* [SPEEKS], *likes* [LYKS].\n- **Rule 2 (/z/):** After voiced vowels & consonants /b, d, g, v, l, m, n, r/ -> *lives* [lɪvz / LIVZ], *plays* [PLAYZ], *reads* [REEDZ].\n- **Rule 3 (/ɪz/ or /əz/ — Extra Syllable!):** After sibilant sounds /s, z, ʃ, tʃ, dʒ/ -> *watches* [ˈwɒtʃɪz / WOTCH-iz], *teaches* [TEECH-iz], *fixes* [FIKS-iz].",
      "examples": [
        "I live in London, but my brother lives (/z/) in Manchester.",
        "She teaches (/ɪz/) English at the university.",
        "He studies (/z/) computer science and works (/s/) at a tech startup.",
        "They speak three languages fluently."
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "He/She/It ALWAYS takes the 'S' (He workS, She liveS, It rainS).",
        "Sibilant /ch, sh, ss, x/ adds a whole new syllable: Watch -> Watch-IZ, Teach -> Teach-IZ."
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "In global business English, forgetting the 3rd person singular '-s' is the single most common grammatical slip by non-native speakers, so mastering it early builds immediate professional credibility."
      ]
    },
