    "id": "en-u4-l4",
    "unit": "Present-Tense Basics & Sentence Structure",
    "level": "A1",
    "objective": "Apply Adverbs of Frequency (always, usually, often, sometimes, rarely/hardly ever, never) and master their exact syntactic placement: BEFORE main verbs but AFTER the verb 'to be'.",
    "prerequisites": [
      "en-u4-l3"
    ],
    "warmup": {
      "srsPull": true,
      "reviewFocus": [
        "en-u4-l2",
        "en-u4-l3"
      ]
    },
    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "**Adverbs of Frequency** describe how often an action happens. Their placement in an English sentence follows two absolute rules.\n\n1. **The Spectrum of Frequency:**\n- **always** (100%) — *I always drink water with meals.*\n- **usually / normally** (80-90%) — *She usually takes the train.*\n- **often / frequently** (60-70%) — *We often eat at this pub.*\n- **sometimes** (40-50%) — *I sometimes work from home on Fridays.*\n- **rarely / seldom / hardly ever** (10-20%) — *He rarely drinks alcohol.*\n- **never** (0%) — *They never arrive late.*\n\n2. **THE 2 GOLDEN PLACEMENT RULES:**\n- **RULE 1: BEFORE Normal Main Verbs:**\n  * Subject + **ADVERB** + Main Verb\n  * *She **always drinks** tea in the morning.*\n  * *Tom **never eats** fast food.*\n  * *We **usually start** work at 9 AM.*\n- **RULE 2: AFTER the Verb 'TO BE' (am / is / are / was / were):**\n  * Subject + **TO BE** + **ADVERB**\n  * *He **is always** on time.* (❌ NOT 'He always is on time'!).\n  * *They **are never** late for class.*\n  * *I **am usually** tired on Monday mornings.*\n\n3. **'Never' and 'Rarely' are Already Negative!**\n- Never use a double negative with 'never':\n  * Correct: **He never drinks coffee.** ✓\n  * Blunder: **He doesn't never drink coffee.** ❌.",
      "examples": [
        "Alex always arrives early, and he is always prepared.",
        "We rarely eat out during the week because we love cooking at home.",
        "She is never in her office on Wednesday afternoons.",
        "Do you usually commute by bicycle?"
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "Before the Main Verb (Always work, Never sleep).",
        "After the Verb TO BE (Is always, Are never).",
        "Never + Affirmative Verb = Negative meaning (He never drinks)."
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "In British communication, understatement is prized: saying 'I rarely do that' or 'That's hardly ever the case' sounds more natural and polished than dramatic absolutes."
      ]
    },
