    "objective": "Form Past Simple negatives (didn't + base verb) and questions (Did + subject + base verb / Wh- question words) without double-past errors.",
    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "In English, forming negatives and questions in the past simple uses the auxiliary verb **DID** (the past of do):\n\n1. Past Simple Negatives (didn't + BASE VERB):\n   - Formula: **Subject + didn't (did not) + BASE VERB**\n   - **The Golden Rule**: The auxiliary 'did' already carries the past tense, so the main verb returns to its **BASE / INFINITIVE FORM**!\n     - *Correct*: *I didn't go to the party.* (NOT: *I didn't went*!)\n     - *Correct*: *She didn't see the movie.* (NOT: *She didn't saw*!)\n     - *Correct*: *We didn't have enough time.* (NOT: *We didn't had*!)\n\n2. Past Simple Questions (Did + Subject + BASE VERB):\n   - **Yes/No Questions**:\n     - *'Did you enjoy the trip?'* -> *'Yes, I did.' / 'No, I didn't.'*\n     - *'Did they visit the castle?'* -> *'Yes, they did.'*\n   - **Wh- Questions (Information Questions)**:\n     - Formula: **Wh- word + did + subject + BASE VERB**\n     - *'Where did you stay in London?'* (NOT: *Where did you stayed?*)\n     - *'What time did the train arrive?'*\n     - *'How did you get there?'*\n     - *'What did you buy at the market?'*",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Did you see the fireworks over Edinburgh Castle last night?",
          "reading": "Did you see (base verb after did).",
          "translation": "Did you see the fireworks over Edinburgh Castle last night?"
        },
        {
          "target": "We didn't take the bus; we walked along the Royal Mile instead.",
          "reading": "didn't take (base verb) ... walked (positive past verb).",
          "translation": "We didn't take the bus; we walked along the Royal Mile instead."
        },
        {
          "target": "Where did you eat dinner on Saturday evening?",
          "reading": "Where did you eat (wh- question structure).",
          "translation": "Where did you eat dinner on Saturday evening?"
        }
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "DID is a past-tense sponge — it soaks up the past, leaving the main verb in its BASE form!",
        "Never say 'didn't went' or 'did you saw' — double past is a no-no!"
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "In short answers to questions, native speakers naturally repeat the auxiliary: 'Did you like it?' -> 'Yes, I did!' / 'No, I didn't!' rather than just saying 'Yes' or 'No'."
      ]
    },
