    "objective": "Use past time adverbials (yesterday, last night, two days ago, the other day) and sequential narrative connectors (in the beginning, then, after that, suddenly, in the end, finally) to construct engaging stories.",
    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "To tell compelling, structured stories in English, you need chronological time anchors and transitional narrative bridges:\n\n1. Past Time Expressions:\n   - **Yesterday** / **Yesterday morning** / **Yesterday afternoon**\n   - **Last night** (NOT: *yesterday night*!)\n   - **Last weekend** / **Last week** / **Last month** / **Last summer**\n   - **Two days ago** / **Three weeks ago** / **A few moments ago** (*ago* comes AFTER the time duration!)\n   - **The other day** (a few days ago)\n   - **In 2024** / **On Friday**\n\n2. Sequential Story Connectors & Narrative Transitions:\n   - **In the beginning / At first** (*'At first, it was raining heavily...'*)\n   - **First of all** / **Firstly**\n   - **Then / Next** (*'Then we climbed up to the viewpoint...'*)\n   - **After that / Afterward** (*'After that, we had lunch at a cozy pub.'*)\n   - **Suddenly / All of a sudden** (*'Suddenly, the sky cleared and the sun came out!'*)\n   - **In the end / Finally** (*'In the end, we returned to our hotel exhausted but happy.'*)",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Two days ago we arrived in Edinburgh by train.",
          "reading": "Two days ago (duration + ago) past time marker.",
          "translation": "Two days ago we arrived in Edinburgh by train."
        },
        {
          "target": "At first it was foggy, but suddenly the clouds lifted and we saw the castle.",
          "reading": "At first ... suddenly (narrative transition dynamic).",
          "translation": "At first it was foggy, but suddenly the clouds lifted and we saw the castle."
        },
        {
          "target": "After that, we walked down the Royal Mile and finally stopped for afternoon tea.",
          "reading": "After that ... finally (sequential story progression).",
          "translation": "After that, we walked down the Royal Mile and finally stopped for afternoon tea."
        }
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "Say 'LAST NIGHT', never 'yesterday night'!",
        "AGO follows the time: 'Two days AGO', 'Ten minutes AGO'.",
        "At first -> Then -> After that -> Suddenly -> In the end!"
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "In British storytelling, using words like 'all of a sudden' or 'out of the blue' adds dramatic pacing when recounting unexpected weather changes or travel encounters."
      ]
    },
