    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "The linguistic and analytical revolution in Cambridge philosophy:\n- **Bertrand Russell (1872–1970 - Cambridge - 1950 Nobel Prize in Literature)**:\n  - Co-authored the monumental 3-volume **«Principia Mathematica» (1910–1913)** with Alfred North Whitehead, attempting to derive all mathematical truths from formal symbolic logic.\n  - Formulated the **Theory of Definite Descriptions** (*\"The present King of France is bald\"*) and **Russell's Paradox** in set theory.\n- **Ludwig Wittgenstein (1889–1951 - Cambridge)**:\n  - The only philosopher in history to produce two distinct world-changing philosophical systems:\n    1. **Early Wittgenstein**: **«Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus» (1921)**:\n       - Picture Theory of Language (*\"The world is everything that is the case\"*); concluding with the immortal aphorism: **«Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent»** (*Wovon man nicht sprechen kann, darüber muss man schweigen*).\n    2. **Late Wittgenstein**: **«Philosophical Investigations» (1953)**:\n       - Rejecting formal logic for everyday language: **Language-Games (Sprachspiele)** and **Forms of Life (Lebensformen)**—meaning is not an abstract object, but **meaning is use** in social practice (*\"Don't think, look!\"*).",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Bertrand Russell's logicism in Principia Mathematica and Ludwig Wittgenstein's language-games in Cambridge revolutionized analytical philosophy and the understanding of linguistic meaning.",
          "reading": "Bertrand Russell's logicism in Principia Mathematica...",
          "translation": "Bertrand Russell's logicism in Principia Mathematica and Ludwig Wittgenstein's language-games in Cambridge revolutionized analytical philosophy and the understanding of linguistic meaning."
        }
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "Bertrand Russell (Principia Mathematica & 1950 Nobel Prize in Literature)! Ludwig Wittgenstein (Tractatus: \"Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent\")! Philosophical Investigations: Meaning is Use!"
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "Wittgenstein gave away his entire enormous inherited steel fortune in Vienna after World War I to live as an elementary school teacher and hospital orderly before returning to Cambridge."
      ]
    },
