      "explanation": "William Morris (1834–1896 - London) and the Arts and Crafts revolution:\n- **William Morris**:\n  - Poet, designer, socialist, and craftsman; founder of **Morris & Co.** and the **Arts and Crafts Movement**.\n- Core Ideology & Anti-Industrial Humanism:\n  - Championed **handcrafted artisan dignity** against soul-crushing Victorian industrial mass production and alienation (inspired by John Ruskin's *The Stones of Venice*);\n  - Famous Design Axiom: **«Have nothing in your houses that you do not know to be useful, or believe to be beautiful.»**\n- Masterpiece Architecture & Printing:\n  - **The Red House (Bexleyheath - 1859 - with architect Philip Webb)**: Vernacular red brick residential masterpiece rejecting neoclassical stucco for exposed craft masonry, hand-carved oak, and stained glass.\n  - **The Kelmscott Press (1891)**: Reviving medieval woodcut typography with the landmark *Kelmscott Chaucer*.\n  - Iconic wallpaper and textile patterns: *Strawberry Thief* (1883) and *Willow Bough*.",
