      "explanation": "Benjamin Britten (1913–1976 - Lowestoft, Suffolk & Aldeburgh) and the 20th-century operatic summit:\n- **Benjamin Britten (Baron Britten of Aldeburgh)**:\n  - The foremost British composer of the 20th century; lifelong pacifist and partner to tenor Peter Pears; founder of the **Aldeburgh Festival** on the Suffolk coast.\n- Monumental Masterpieces:\n  - **«War Requiem» op. 66 (Premiered May 30, 1962 for the consecration of the rebuilt Coventry Cathedral)**:\n    - Masterpiece of moral reconciliation; interweaving the Latin Requiem Mass for the dead with the visceral anti-war poetry of Wilfred Owen (killed in action 1918);\n    - Written for 3 vocal soloists from wartime enemy nations: Russian soprano Galina Vishnevskaya, British tenor Peter Pears, and German baritone Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau;\n    - Famously prefaced with Owen's words: **«My subject is War, and the pity of War. The Poetry is in the pity... All a poet can do today is warn.»**\n  - **«Peter Grimes» (1945)**: The opera that relaunched English opera onto the world stage;\n  - **«The Young Person's Guide to the Orchestra» (1945)** on a theme of Purcell.",
