      "explanation": "Arthur Miller (1915–2005 - New York & Connecticut) and the moral anatomy of the American Dream:\n- **Arthur Miller**:\n  - The foremost moral dramatist of 20th-century American theater; recipient of the **Pulitzer Prize for Drama (1949)** and the Prince of Asturias Award.\n- Monumental Masterpieces:\n  - **«Death of a Salesman» (1949 - Pulitzer Prize & Tony Award)**:\n    - Follows aging, exhausted traveling salesman **Willy Loman**, who has spent his life chasing the illusion that being \"well-liked\" and selling personality guarantees wealth and dignity in corporate America;\n    - As his mental sanity unravels, his wife Linda Loman delivers the immortal moral defense of ordinary human dignity: **«Willy Loman never made a lot of money... He's not a great man. But he's a human being, and a terrible thing is happening to him. So attention must be paid. He's not to be allowed to fall into his grave like an old dog. Attention, attention must finally be paid to such a person.»**;\n    - Willy commits suicide by car crash so his son Biff can collect $20,000 in life insurance money;\n  - **«The Crucible» (1953)**: Dramatizing the 1692 Salem Witch Trials as an impassioned allegory of Senator Joseph McCarthy's anti-communist blacklisting hysterias in America (*John Proctor refusing to sign away his name: \"Because it is my name! Because I cannot have another in my life!\"*).",
