    "objective": "The Limits of Language & Language-Games: Ludwig Wittgenstein (Ludwig Wittgenstein - Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus 1921: «Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent», Philosophical Investigations 1953: Language-games (Sprachspiele), Meaning as Use, the Beetle in the Box, Cambridge).",
    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "Ludwig Wittgenstein (1889–1951 - Vienna & Cambridge) and the two revolutionary linguistic philosophies of the 20th century:\n- **Ludwig Wittgenstein**:\n  - Born to one of the wealthiest industrial families in Austria; gave away his entire fortune; studied under Bertrand Russell at Cambridge; the only philosopher in history to create **two completely distinct, opposing world-historical philosophical systems**.\n- The First Philosophy: **Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921 - Composed in WWI trenches)**:\n  - **The Picture Theory of Language (Bildtheorie)**: Language pictures the logical state of affairs in the physical world ($\"The world is all that is the case\"$);\n  - Proposition 5.6: **«The limits of my language mean the limits of my world»**;\n  - Proposition 7: **«Wovon man nicht sprechen kann, darüber muss man schweigen»** (*\"Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent\"*);\n- The Second Philosophy: **Philosophical Investigations (Philosophische Untersuchungen - 1953)**:\n  - Overthrew his own Tractatus: Language is not a rigid mathematical mirror, but a collection of dynamic **Language-Games (Sprachspiele)** embedded in human social activities (*forms of life / Lebensformen*);\n  - **Meaning is Use**: *\"The meaning of a word is its use in the language\"*\n  - **The Beetle in the Box**: Private mental sensations cannot provide the public objective criteria for linguistic meaning.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Ludwig Wittgenstein transformed philosophy twice: with the Tractatus logic of 1921 and the Language-Games of Philosophical Investigations in 1953.",
          "reading": "Ludwig Wittgenstein transformed...",
          "translation": "Ludwig Wittgenstein transformed philosophy twice: with the Tractatus logic of 1921 and the Language-Games of Philosophical Investigations in 1953."
        }
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "Ludwig Wittgenstein (Viennese-Cambridge Linguistic Titan)! Tractatus 1921 («The limits of my language mean the limits of my world» & Proposition 7)! Philosophical Investigations 1953 (Language-Games / Sprachspiele)! Meaning is Use! Beetle in the Box!"
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "After publishing the *Tractatus* in 1921, believing he had solved all philosophical problems, Wittgenstein quit philosophy to work as an elementary school teacher in rural Austrian alpine villages for six years before returning to Cambridge."
      ]
    },
